Z||000014||Mach10||09-01-2001||01:51 AM||||Sorry to keep this up, but I can't leave now, it's just getting fun:<P>Ken: When you spin the Amino acid on it's axis, it doesn't become unsuitable for life. What it does do is change the function. The way proteins are structured, it's the shape that determines the function. All flipping the Amino acid to it's D or L configuration does is change the function. In our case, yes, scrambling Amino Acids will result in our death, since the proteins are no longer doing what they are supposed to. However, there are D and L analogues that are both present in our systems. The reason being that they perform different function. <P>Now, as for the test-tube thing, No, they didn't create life. What they did do was prove that the basic elements of life as we know it (certain amino acid chains) can and DO occur naturally with no intervention. What this means is that over time, random interactions between amino-acids can produce living organisms. Don't get me wrong, it's still a long-shot, but the odds are in fact rather good when you consider how impossibly huge the universe is.<P>Tar is not toxic. Some of the compounds are toxic to US. Certain alcohols and such. But remember that "tar" as we know it is the direct result of microbial interaction with dead materials. Yes. Our Fieros run on bacterial **** . Life can and does live in petroleum. The only thing about oil is that there isn't a whole lot of stuff to eat. MOST of terrestrial organisms cannot metabolize raw hydrocarbons in those forms. However, certain single-celled animals can and do.<BR>Now, the primordial tar that you are thinking about would be a slimy mixture of assorted hydro-carbon chains. Not very nutritious to any single or multi-celled animal today, True. However, this would be the place where life STARTS. Any organism that springs up here would be very well adapted to living in that puddle of slime. Afterwards? Evolution starts up, and things start migrating out.<P>Now, to jump in on topic:<P>I don't really get this whole "Government is out to get me" thing. There's LOTS of speculation, but very little proof that anything off-colour is happening. Heresay and Conjecture notwithstanding, does anyone actually have physical evidence that the government is actively trying to manipulate people in the ways mentioned? <P>I will agree that some bad ****  does happen, sometimes. The thing is, though, that the government *IS* run by people. There are good people, and bad people. The bad ones will start disregarding privacy and rights, and the good ones will uphold them. If the government IS up to something BIG, then there is absolutely nothing that you can do to stop them. <P>You know, there are very few other countries in the world with such a widespread conspiracy-theorist movement than the USA. Up here, in Canada, nobody believes that the Government of Canada is up to anything more sinister than slapping us with taxes whenever we turn our backs. Why is this, do you suppose? Is the US truly that different from everyone else? I'm almost tempted to try and snag a grant and do a study for my Cultural Anthro component. <P>Back to this damn dam, without actually going inside, I don't believe that there's any way to know what's going on. Therefore, it's counterproductive to speculate. Is there any way of getting in? Does anyone know anyone "on the inside?" That'd be the best way of finding out what's going on.||||reg||7
