Z||000033||rogergarrison||09-07-2001||05:21 PM||||could be they just dont care if we see them. Like us going out in the woods at nite, we dont care if the animals see us or not. The SR71 had a little glow from atomospheric pressure at high speed. It and the Concorde actually expand almost a couple feet at speed from surface air friction. Its so bad on Concorde that the interior floor is mounted to fuselage on large ball bearings to let it slide as fuselage stretches.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 09-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000034||Raydar||09-07-2001||05:50 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DaRkLoRD:<BR><B>...that's why SDI was built... </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I've heard that SDI -Ronald Reagan's "Star Wars" -was just a smoke screen. Never really existed in any large scale. Was designed to make the Soviets freak out and try to compete, thereby wasting TONS of money. (They *did* go broke, in a manner of speaking.) <BR>While the Russians were looking towards the sky, we were quietly enhancing our submarine tracking ability. Something about a network of undersea cables/listening devices, used to intercept Soviet submarine communications. <BR>Very costly, to be sure, but this is where the money went. NOT Star Wars. <BR>If I am in error about details, or the whole thing for that matter, excuse me. <BR>Quite a few years ago when I read that.<P>I will look up Project Horizon. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 09-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000035||maryjane||09-07-2001||07:11 PM||||SDI (aka star wars) is a very real and expensive project, currently under development by DOD, US Army, USN, and Air Force. It was first proposed by R.Reagan prematurly, the technology was just in infancy at that time and is still a good ways off. As far as diverting funds, there were very little to divert from SDI, as very little was funded by congress. I suspect there was some diversion from other projects to SDI, to keep it going. In recent years, it has become a more viable project and gets it's slice of the DOD budget pie. There are several versions of SDI being developed and a couple are in the test/evaluation phase now. As usual there is squabbling between the AF, Army,& Navy about which one to further develop. It is probably a clear violation of the SALT agreement with the Soviet Union, no matter which version is adapted||||reg||1
